  "text": "Her son died on her lap. She said \"It shall be well,\" and saddled a donkey. Second Kings four. This woman is described as great. Standing. Money. Influence. She is the one who notices Elisha passing through and builds him a room. Bed, table, stool, candlestick. He wants to repay her. She says no. She is fine. He gives her a son anyway. Years later the boy is out in the field, says \"My head, my head,\" and is carried to his mother. And the text says he sat on her knees till noon, and then died. Now watch her. She carries him upstairs. Lays him on the prophet's bed. Shuts the door. And calls for a donkey. Her husband asks why she is going, it is not new moon or sabbath. She says, \"It shall be well.\" She does not tell him the child is dead. She is not asking permission. She is going. She rides to Carmel. Elisha's servant tries to intercept her with polite questions. She goes straight past him. Then she catches Elisha by the feet. And Gehazi moves to shove her off, and Elisha says, \"Let her alone, for her soul is vexed within her.\" And she says, \"As the Lord liveth, and as thy soul liveth, I will not leave thee.\" He got up and followed her.",
